recording alright let's get into  audience questions for the week three  very timely and prescient questions I  think I would say the first one is hey  David are you addicted to your  smartphone  why are smartphone so addicting and how  do you deal with it  we're actually scheduled to have a  really good interview about technology  addiction and digital healthy digital  lifestyles or maybe that's not the best  way to say it but I think you'll you'll  know it when you hear it next week  I don't I don't think I'm addicted to my  smartphone but I am very conscious of  times where my smartphone use has sort  of gotten to a place where I'm not super  comfortable with it that the reason that  smartphones are so addicting is by  design there's basically two elements to  why it's so rewarding not in like an  emotional sense but like to the brain to  keep scrolling through social media and  it basically comes down to two factors  and Cal Newport has written about this  and so of many psychologists and others  I've just recently read Cal's material  on this one is that social media is sort  of and and our smartphones are designed  with a variable reward system and when  you go back to these old animal tests I  don't remember if it was rats or Birds  or whoever but what what scientists  determined was when you set up a system  that rewarded animals with food on a  variable basis where you might press a  button and get no food or get one pellet  or six pallets and it was unpredictable  the animals became way more fixated on  pressing the button than if it was just  when you press it you get half a pellet  and that's it that's how social media  and email and all of that stuff works  the entire notification paradigm did I  do I have anything in there it's like  pulling a slot machine and you might  have like some notifications or you  might have new emails but the emails  might be spam or they might be really  good if you have an online business they  might be messages about a new order that  came in for example or whatever the case  may be  the variable reward  a situation paradigm is one reason why  people keep scrolling and then the other  is that a lot of those variable rewards  relate to social approval so when it  comes to likes Facebook messages  notifications comments on stuff you  posted app messages on Twitter not only  is there the variable reward system but  it's not just did I hit the slot machine  and get some money  it's notifications or rewards that sort  of validate your self-worth on the basis  of how are other people reacting so  that's like the core of why these  devices are so addicting and it's by  design I mean there are whistleblowers  from the tech world who have said this  is exactly how these things were  designed speaking candidly during design  meetings that this is exactly what what  we were trying to do so the way I try to  deal with it is I try to have just very  very particular habits with a lot of  this stuff I've not done the thing where  I uninstall a bunch of apps from my  phone or put software on my computer  that doesn't let me go to social media  websites I've basically because social  media stuff is related to my job because  we do use these platforms for promoting  the show I've established in my workday  a period in the morning and a period in  the afternoon where I will go through  our various social media platforms  respond to messages post stuff etc etc  and I try to just stick to that I make  it deliberate rather than just picking  up the phone and starting to scroll with  no particular objective and that's  really the problem when you pick up the  phone and start scrolling you're not  really looking for any particular piece  of information you're not really  determining I'm going to do ten minutes  of this the way you might say I'm  watching one episode on Netflix or  whatever the case may be so I try to  just be very deliberate and it works it  generally works pretty well if I don't  need my phone nearby like right now I  have it here because I use it to time  interviews that we do on the show and  that sort of sort of thing I keep the  phone away and if there's really an  emergency and someone actually calls me  I'll hear the phone and then I'll go  pick it up so those are sort of my  strategies and I've not found that I'm  having a major problem but I know a lot  of people I mean even over over last  weekend at the Super Bowl party I went  to there were people who were mostly  just looking at their phones and  glancing up at the game it's like this  is a this is a once a year a game I mean  yes okay it's most years for the New  England Patriots that were in the Super  Bowl fine but it's still it's a  once-a-year thing is it really is  Facebook really so interesting that  you're scrolling on Facebook rather than  watching the game so I I think I'm doing  okay big picture and I would recommend  you adopt some of those ideas as well
 

